 "v5_top20_rank_agreement_with_wp2": 1.0,
 "blinding": "batch items contain only item_id and text fields (V1 title; V2 title, target word, occurrence, sense, swap word; V3 abstract; V5 12 central titles + 300-char abstract snippets); item order and batch membership are random; keys/ is private",
 "deviations": [
  "V5 shows the 12 most central papers per cluster (title + first 300 characters of the abstract), as the WP3a task specifies; PLAN.md §3 V5 says 20 central abstracts. Central = smallest squared Euclidean distance of the MiniLM abstract embedding to the cluster's label-mean centroid (outputs/topics/centroids.npz minilm_k100), over U papers of all years, skipping exact duplicate titles/abstracts (resubmissions).",
  "V1 hard cases, genre leads: U has only 22 compound titles whose lead is or contains a genre word (features_v2.lead_is_genre, which also fires on leads such as 'Orthogonal Drift Correction (ODC)'). All genre leads not already in the main sample are taken; the rest of the 50 are single-token leads that are ordinary English words (Title-case alphabetic, wordfreq zipf >= 3.5, e.g. 'Attention', 'Uncertainty', 'Echo'), the coined-name vs generic-word boundary.",
  "V1 main sample design: within each year block, 100 NAME-positive titles allocated as equally as possible across the four orthography classes (paren_acronym has only 1-22 titles per block, so all are taken and the remainder is redistributed), and 100 NAME-negative titles split 50 phrase-colon compound / 50 non-compound so that recall can be estimated where misses are plausible. Design weights N_h/n_h are in the key.",
  "V1 dash hard cases: 25 titles whose separator the detector found as a spaced dash, and 25 titles without a detected separator that contain an unspaced en/em dash or '--'/'---', or a dash with space on one side only (the v1 COLON regex misses e.g. ' --- ' and unspaced em dashes).",
  "V1/V2 items show the raw title (whitespace collapsed), not the NFKC title the detectors read, so annotators see what the authors typed (LaTeX kept).",
  "V1 codebook label set: lead_type adds 'none' for non-compound titles; casing has 4 classes, so the detector's 'mixed' and 'other' both map to 'other'. Auxiliary fields 'lead' (verbatim) and 'note' support adjudication.",
  "V2 population: 2018+ (the D3 slot-model years), opener slots filled under EITHER opener rule: the primary opener after an optional NAME lead without the pre-V2 sense filter (slot_*_raw) or the opener after any compound lead (slot_*_al, API.md deviation 3). This covers both candidate primary definitions and the 8 sense-filtered titles. A title filling two slots is assigned by preferring the primary opener over the any-lead opener, then the order improve, reveal, exploit, study_adj. Within each slot x variant x era cell, items are allocated equally across slot words (capped at availability) so that per-word sense precision, the PLAN drop rule (< 0.85), is estimable.",
  "V2 swap test is symmetric: an LLM-favoured target gets the slot's reference neutral word (Improving / Understanding / Exploiting / systematic); a neutral target gets the reference LLM-favoured word (Enhancing / Unveiling / Leveraging / comprehensive). References are the first-listed words in PLAN.md D3. Annotators are not told which word of a pair is LLM-favoured.",
  "V3 strata: year (2024, 2025, 2026) x 4 fingerprint categories (F0; F24 only; F26 or title typography without F24; F24 and F26/typography), 25 per cell, finer than F0/F1 so that component-level convergence can be read. The rubric does not name the fingerprint words or typography, to avoid a mechanically induced correlation.",
  "V5 adds an auxiliary integer 'lm_papers' (how many of the 12 shown papers are centrally about language models) next to the required label and y/n flag, for comparison with the >50% regex-share cluster rule."
 ]
}